      "value": "code_lab"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Execute a numerical scan to test the Algebraic Variety Hypothesis regarding solution space rigidity in small feedforward networks. The plan involves: (1) generating a synthetic system of w algebraic constraints (polynomial equations) that form a zero-dimensional variety when w ≥ d (dimension of input space); (2) training small MLPs with varying hidden widths N to satisfy these constraints on training points; (3) evaluating generalization error on held-out test points; (4) identifying the minimum width N_c(w) achieving zero violation (max abs error ≤ 1e-3); (5) plotting N_c(w) vs w to distinguish between constant (rigid), polynomial (fat), or logarithmic scaling. The experiment will use PyTorch for network training and numpy for constraint generation."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom ML experiment)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "If the Algebraic Variety Hypothesis holds, N_c(w) will remain constant (small finite value) for w beyond a threshold, indicating a rigid solution space. If the Approximation Theory Hypothesis holds, N_c(w) will grow polynomially with w. The Landscape Conditioning Hypothesis predicts logarithmic growth. The scan will reveal which scaling law fits the empirical data."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "prediction_inputs"
    },
